While an assistive technology consumes the accessibility tree,
Ladybird keeps each node’s complete data between builds, recomputes
its bounds, text geometry and focus on every build, and drops the
rest on the changes that can reach it. The other engines keep their
accessibility objects themselves and update only the parts marked
dirty. Blink’s AXObjectCacheImpl marks AXObjects dirty from its
mutation handlers, and AXTreeSerializer::SerializeChanges() sends only
those. WebKit’s AXIsolatedTree::updateNodeProperties() updates just
the properties a change requests, and queues whole-node updates only
for broader changes. Gecko’s DocAccessible::QueueCacheUpdate() records
the CacheDomain bits a change touches, and BundleFieldsForCache()
recomputes only those domains.
